UNIT parse_sig
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs parseSig — the strict recursive-descent SIG reader that shares parseType's
  grammar (the header comment above reader); reproduce it exactly
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG parse_sig(s: text) -> json
ENVELOPE s is any well-formed text. Text outside the grammar throws, and that throw is IN scope as an observation (R1, R2,
  R5, R6, R7, R8); the throw message ("bad SIG: …" with a position) is not graded.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read one SIG line into a map with three entries: name (text), args (a list of argument maps, in order) and result
  (a type tree). An argument map has name (text), type (a type tree) and, for a rest parameter only, rest = true. A type
  tree is a map whose entry k names its kind: k = a SCALAR name alone, or k = "union" with alts (a list of trees), "tuple"
  with items (a list of trees), "list" with item (a tree), "map" with key and val (trees), "record" with fields (a list of
  two-element lists [field name, tree]). No other entries appear.
BEHAVIOR
R1 s must be, as a whole, one SIG of the SIG grammar below; anything else -> throws. The tokens are IDENT and the literals
  ( ) [ ] , : | -> and ... (each literal exactly as spelled: "- >" and ". .." are not tokens). WHITESPACE is optional
  before, between and after any tokens — so "list [text]", "... xs", "f (a :text)->text" and a SIG broken over lines are
  all accepted — and it is required only where two IDENTs meet, because an IDENT is read longest first ("pubfn" is one
  IDENT). No other character is ignored: there is no comment form, so a "#" anywhere throws.
R2 PREFIX is the IDENT token sequence pub fn, or export default function, any WHITESPACE between its words. It is read as a
  prefix only when another IDENT (the function name) follows it; it occurs at most once. Otherwise no prefix is read and the
  first IDENT is the function name: "pub(fn: text) -> text" is a function named pub, "export(default: text) -> text" one
  named export, "pub fn fn(a: text) -> text" one named fn; "pub fn(a: text) -> text", "pub fn" and "pub fn pub fn f() ->
  none" throw
PINNED grammar SIG = [PREFIX] IDENT "(" [PARAM ("," PARAM)*] ")" ("->" | ":") TYPE ; PARAM = ["..."] IDENT ":" TYPE CITED canon.mjs header comment (sig, param) — no trailing or empty PARAM, so "f(a: text,)" and "f(,)" throw
PINNED grammar TYPE = ALT ("|" ALT)* ; ALT = SCALAR | "tuple" "[" TYPE ("," TYPE)* "]" | "list" "[" TYPE "]" | "map" "[" TYPE "," TYPE "]" | "record" "[" IDENT ":" TYPE ("," IDENT ":" TYPE)* "]" CITED canon.mjs header comment (type, alt) — every bracket form needs at least one part and list / map exactly one / two
PINNED grammar IDENT = an ASCII letter or underscore, then any run of ASCII letters, digits and underscores, read longest first CITED canon.mjs ident — "2f" and "fé" throw; keywords are not reserved, so pub, fn and text are ordinary identifiers where an IDENT is expected
R3 The name entry is the function IDENT. Each PARAM becomes an argument map in order; a leading "..." marks it a rest
  parameter and adds rest = true.
R4 TYPE gives a union tree (alts in order) when it has two or more ALTs, else the single ALT's tree. The members of a
  union are never unions themselves, and a nested TYPE inside brackets is its own tree. A SCALAR ALT gives k = that name;
  the tuple form gives items, list item, map key and val, record fields as [IDENT, tree] pairs, all in order.
R5 Unreachable alternatives -> throws: within one TYPE (at any nesting level), an ALT that an EARLIER ALT of the same TYPE
  covers (TABLE COVERS). A later ALT never covers an earlier one, so "text | json" is legal while "json | text" throws; and
  spacing never matters. COVERS is structural: "list[json] | list[text]", "tuple[json, text] | tuple[text, text]",
  "map[json, json] | map[text, text]", "list[text | none] | list[text]" and "record[a: text] | record[a: text, b: none]"
  throw, while "list[text] | list[text | none]", "tuple[json, json] | tuple[text]", "record[a: text, b: none] |
  record[a: text]", "list[text] | tuple[text]" and "json | map[json, text]" are legal. CITED canon.mjs typeKey, inJson,
  covers (FINDINGS #65 #74 #79)
TABLE COVERS — A covers B (A, B type trees) exactly when at least one row holds; nothing else covers (a list never covers a
  tuple, a map never a record, and only json or a union holding json covers json); used by R5
  SAME     A and B are the same type up to order: the same kind and, recursively, the same members, where the alts of a union
           and the fields of a record (a field = its name, compared exactly, and its tree) compare as SETS, while tuple
           items, the list item and the map key and val compare in order
  JSON     A is the scalar json and B is JSON-SHAPED (TABLE JSON_SHAPED)
  LIST     both are lists and A's item covers B's item
  TUPLE    both are tuples with the same number of items and each A item covers the B item at the same position
  MAP      both are maps, A's key covers B's key and A's val covers B's val
  RECORD   both are records and every field of A has a field of the same name in B whose tree A's field tree covers (B may
           have more fields than A)
  A-UNION  A is a union and some alt of A covers B
  B-UNION  B is a union and A covers every alt of B
TABLE JSON_SHAPED — a type tree B -> whether json covers it; used by COVERS
  none, bool, f64, text, json -> yes
  i64, u64, bytes             -> no
  list[X]                     -> yes exactly when X is json-shaped
  map[K, V]                   -> yes exactly when K is the scalar text itself (not json, not a union) and V is json-shaped
  tuple, record               -> yes exactly when every item (every field's tree) is json-shaped
  a union                     -> yes exactly when every alt is json-shaped
R6 Uniqueness -> throws: two PARAMs with the same IDENT, or two fields of one record with the same IDENT (compared exactly,
  so a and A differ).
R7 A rest parameter anywhere but the last PARAM -> throws (two rest parameters included).
R8 Nesting cap -> throws: a composite ALT (tuple list map record) nested inside more than 15 enclosing composite ALTs. A
  composite at the top of a PARAM's TYPE or the result TYPE has depth 1, one inside its brackets depth 2, and so on; depth
  16 is accepted, 17 throws. Unions add no depth, and siblings do not add up: each PARAM and the result start again at 0.
PINNED constant MAX-DEPTH = 16 CITED canon.mjs MAX_DEPTH (FINDINGS #78) — the deepest accepted composite nesting
PINNED charset WHITESPACE = space, tab, carriage return, line feed (hexadecimal 20 09 0D 0A) CITED canon.mjs reader ws — nothing else: vertical tab, form feed, no-break space, zero width no-break space, next line and the line separator all throw
PINNED names SCALAR = text bytes bool none i64 u64 f64 json CITED canon.mjs TYPES, the closed type set; case-sensitive
INVARIANT every accepted s is ASCII; name is an IDENT; argument names are distinct; at most one argument carries rest and it
  is the last; every list tree has an item, every map tree a key and a val; a union has at least two alts, none covered (R5)
  by an earlier one; no tree nests composites deeper than MAX-DEPTH.
ORACLE value -> oracles/parse_sig.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 ab2348b66cc3ca41f40bb5fc7ab9fd049458eb867fffb3eb0394280b7bf36d5c
SCOPE IN: R1–R8 over every well-formed text. OUT: non-text input (not representable under the SIG).
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 (selfhost pinned authority, round 5), cli/sir/canon.mjs: parseSig, reader (sig, prefix,
  type, alt, ident, ws, MAX_DEPTH), typeKey, sameType, inJson, covers, TYPES. Round 5 re-cut after FINDINGS #74 #78 #79:
  coverage is structural (TABLE COVERS), json covers tuple and record of json-shaped parts, and the nesting cap is 16.
